-- Migration 33: repair flashcard.fsrs_json, which silently discarded writes.
--
-- SurrealDB 2.7.0 empties object values on a SCHEMAFULL table when the
-- field has no nested FIELD definitions, UNLESS the definition is marked
-- FLEXIBLE. Migration 32 declared:
--
--     DEFINE FIELD fsrs_json ON TABLE flashcard TYPE option<object>;
--
-- which accepts the write, reports OK, and stores `{}`. Every FSRS grade
-- therefore computed a correct schedule, returned it to the caller, wrote
-- an empty object, and the card stayed due forever. Found by running the
-- review flow on-device, not by a test - tests/test_review_store.py stubs
-- repo_query, so the write path was never exercised.
--
-- Reproduced in isolation on 2.7.0, SCHEMAFULL table, object value written,
-- testing both whether a *missing* field is accepted and whether content
-- survives:
--
--     TYPE option<object>                 -> NONE ok, content {}   (lost)
--     TYPE object, no nested defs          -> NONE ok, content {}   (lost)
--     TYPE any                            -> NONE ok, content {}   (lost)
--     TYPE option<object> DEFAULT NONE    -> NONE ok, content {}   (lost)
--     FLEXIBLE TYPE object                -> NONE REJECTED, content ok
--     FLEXIBLE TYPE option<object>        -> NONE ok, content ok   <- this
--     FLEXIBLE TYPE any                   -> NONE ok, content ok
--
-- Both halves matter. FLEXIBLE is what stops the content being emptied,
-- but plain `FLEXIBLE TYPE object` rejects NONE, and _prepare_save_data
-- omits every None field, so a card created before its first review has no
-- fsrs_json at all. `option<>` is what allows that absence.
--
-- FLEXIBLE rather than explicit nested fields on purpose: the dict shape
-- belongs to py-fsrs, not to us, so pinning the schema to today's seven
-- keys would break the first time that library adds or renames one.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE fsrs_json ON TABLE flashcard FLEXIBLE TYPE option<object>;